.\" ctriple(1), cantriple-socketcan
.TH CTRIPLE 1 "October 2026" "cantriple-socketcan 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ctriple \- switch, inspect and bring up a CAN Triple 2.0 as a SocketCAN adapter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ctriple
.RB [ status ]
.RB [ \-\-unit
.IR ID ]
.PP
.B ctriple socketcan
.RB [ \-\-persist ]
.RB [ \-\-unit
.IR ID ]
.RB [ \-\-password
.IR PASSWORD ]
.PP
.B ctriple manager
.RB [ \-\-persist ]
.RB [ \-\-unit
.IR ID ]
.RB [ \-\-password
.IR PASSWORD ]
.PP
.B ctriple up
.RB [ \-\-bitrate
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-dbitrate
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-listen\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-loopback ]
.RB [ \-\-berr\-reporting ]
.RB [ \-\-bus
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-unit
.IR ID ]
.PP
.B ctriple down
.RB [ \-\-bus
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-unit
.IR ID ]
.PP
.B ctriple
.BR \-\-version " | " \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
A CAN Triple 2.0 is one of two USB devices at a time:
.TP
.B the Device Manager's port
a USB serial port
.RI ( /dev/ttyACM N )
speaking the Device Manager's protocol, used to configure the unit, update its
firmware and watch it. Every unit starts as this unless told otherwise.
.TP
.B the SocketCAN adapter
a gs_usb CAN adapter, which the Linux kernel's own
.B gs_usb
driver turns into three SocketCAN network interfaces, one per bus. This
package's udev rule names them
.BI ct UNIT \-can1\fR,
.BI ct UNIT \-can2
and
.BI ct UNIT \-can3
for the unit's CAN1, CAN2 and CAN3, where
.I UNIT
is the last six characters of the unit's serial number in lower case (the
serial number is the MCU ID the Device Manager shows, e.g.
.B 2035344746325012004F002B
gives
.BR ct4f002b\-can1 ).
.PP
.B ctriple
moves a unit between the two, says which each unit is, and brings the
interfaces up and down. The interfaces are ordinary SocketCAN interfaces:
anything
.B ctriple up
does can also be done with
.BR ip\-link (8),
and every SocketCAN program (candump, cansend, python\-can, SavvyCAN) uses
them as it uses any other. See
.BR cantriple\-socketcan (7)
for the setup, what the unit supports, and how it behaves.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
The default. One line for every unit plugged in (or for the one
.B \-\-unit
names):
.RS
.TP
in SocketCAN mode
each bus, its interface, and whether the interface is up
.RB ( "ip link set up" ,
not whether a bus is connected). "no interfaces: gs_usb has not taken it"
means the kernel has not bound
.B gs_usb
to the unit: the udev rule is missing or masked, or the
.B gs_usb
module is unavailable.
.TP
on the Device Manager's port
the serial port, the firmware version, and the mode the unit starts in at
every power-up. "fell back from the SocketCAN adapter" means the unit was the
adapter, set to start as it or asked to be it elsewhere, and gave this PC the
Manager's port because nothing here used the adapter (see
.B FALLBACK
in
.BR cantriple\-socketcan (7)).
"without SocketCAN mode, or before 2.0.10" means the firmware cannot say which
mode it starts in: before 2.0.7 it has no SocketCAN mode at all; 2.0.7 to 2.0.9
have it but cannot keep it across power-ups.
.RE
.TP
.B socketcan
Turn a unit on the Device Manager's port into the SocketCAN adapter. The unit
acknowledges, leaves the USB bus for a quarter of a second and comes back as
the adapter; the serial port disappears, and with it any Device Manager
session. Waits up to six seconds for the interfaces, then prints them. With
.BR \-\-persist ,
the unit also starts as the adapter at every power-up from now on. Asks for
the CAN Viewer password if the unit has one (see
.BR \-\-password ).
Needs firmware 2.0.7 or later (2.0.10 for
.BR \-\-persist ).
.TP
.B manager
Turn a unit in SocketCAN mode back into the Device Manager's port. Its
interfaces disappear (the kernel removes them as the unit leaves the bus); the
buses go back to the unit's own configuration. Waits up to six seconds for the
serial port. Sent as a USB control request, so the unit's
.I /dev/bus/usb
node must be writable (the udev rule gives that to the user at the seat;
otherwise use sudo). With
.BR \-\-persist ,
the unit also starts as the Manager's port from now on. On a unit already on
the Manager's port,
.B manager \-\-persist
changes only the mode it starts in, and does not re-enumerate it.
.TP
.B up
Bring the unit's interfaces up: each is taken down, given its bit rates and
modes, and brought up. Needs root
.RB ( CAP_NET_ADMIN ).
Acts on every unit in SocketCAN mode, or the one
.B \-\-unit
names, and on every bus, or those
.B \-\-bus
names. The bit rates are, in order of preference:
.BR \-\-bitrate " / " \-\-dbitrate ,
then
.BR CAN \fIn\fB_BITRATE " / " CAN \fIn\fB_DBITRATE
for that bus in
.IR /etc/default/cantriple\-socketcan ,
then
.BR BITRATE " / " DBITRATE
there. A data rate makes the bus CAN FD. The
.B OPTIONS
in that file are added to every bus. A bus with no bit rate anywhere is an
error.
.TP
.B down
Take the unit's interfaces down
.RB ( "ip link set down" ),
every bus or those
.B \-\-bus
names. Needs root. A bus taken down goes back to the unit's own configuration
at once.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-unit " ID"
Act on the unit whose serial number ends with
.I ID
(any length, upper or lower case;
the six characters in the interface names are enough). Needed by
.BR socketcan " and " manager
when more than one unit is in the mode they act on; without it,
.BR status ", " up " and " down
act on every unit.
.TP
.B \-\-persist
With
.BR socketcan " or " manager :
also keep the mode as the one the unit starts in at every power-up (kept in
the unit's FRAM; firmware 2.0.10 or later). Without it, a mode lasts until the
unit restarts.
.TP
.BI \-\-password " PASSWORD"
The unit's CAN Viewer password. SocketCAN mode reads and writes the buses raw,
as the Device Manager's CAN Viewer does, so the same password guards it. Only
used if the unit refuses without it; when it is not given,
.B ctriple
asks for it on the terminal, or takes it from
.BR CTRIPLE_PASSWORD .
A password proved stays proved until the unit leaves the USB bus. The unit
learns nothing of the password but a challenge's answer.
.TP
.BI \-\-bitrate " N"
.RB ( up )
The nominal (arbitration) bit rate, in bit/s, for every bus chosen. Linux works
out the timing from the unit's limits, with its usual sample point (87.5 %
up to 500 kbit/s, 80 % above, 75 % above 800 kbit/s).
.TP
.BI \-\-dbitrate " N"
.RB ( up )
The CAN FD data phase's bit rate, in bit/s, which turns CAN FD on. 1 to 8
Mbit/s; the unit's transceivers are rated to 5 Mbit/s, and 8 works on short
buses with few nodes.
.TP
.B \-\-listen\-only
.RB ( up )
Receive only: the unit never acknowledges a frame, never transmits, never
sends an error frame. Safe on a bus whose bit rate is not known.
.TP
.B \-\-loopback
.RB ( up )
Loop back inside the unit's CAN controller: frames sent come back as received
frames, and nothing reaches the pins. A test of the USB path and the
controller with nothing wired.
.TP
.B \-\-berr\-reporting
.RB ( up )
Report each bus error (a stuff, form, acknowledgement, bit or CRC error) as an
error frame, the latest one at most every millisecond a bus. The changes of
bus state (warning, passive, bus-off) are reported whether or not this is on.
.TP
.BI \-\-bus " LIST"
.RB ( up ", " down )
The buses to act on, by the unit's own numbering: a comma-separated list of 1,
2 and 3. All three by default.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done. For
.BR status :
at least one unit was found.
.TP
.B 1
It could not be done; the reason is on standard error (see
.BR DIAGNOSTICS ).
For
.BR status :
no unit was found.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was not understood.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CTRIPLE_PASSWORD
The CAN Viewer password, when
.B \-\-password
is not given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/cantriple\-socketcan
The bit rates
.B ctriple up
falls back on, and the automatic bring-up's settings; see
.BR cantriple\-socketcan (7).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-cantriple\-gs\-usb.rules
Binds
.B gs_usb
to the unit, names its interfaces, and runs the automatic bring-up.
.TP
.I /dev/ttyACM*
The Device Manager's port: readable and writable by the
.B dialout
group, which
.B status
and
.B socketcan
need.
.TP
.I /dev/bus/usb/*/*
The unit in SocketCAN mode, which
.B manager
writes its request to.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B no CAN Triple 2.0 found
No unit is plugged in, or none matches
.BR \-\-unit .
A CAN Triple (1.x) is not a CAN Triple 2.0 and cannot be an adapter.
.TP
.BI "is already the SocketCAN adapter" " / " "is already the Manager port"
The unit is in the mode asked for.
.TP
.BI "N units ...: choose one with \-\-unit"
More than one unit could be meant; the message lists their IDs.
.TP
.B this firmware has no SocketCAN mode
The unit's firmware is older than 2.0.7. Update it with the Device Manager.
.TP
.B this firmware cannot keep the mode
.B \-\-persist
on firmware older than 2.0.10.
.TP
.B the unit could not keep the mode
The unit could not write its FRAM. Nothing was changed.
.TP
.BR "the unit has a CAN Viewer password" ", " "wrong CAN Viewer password"
Give the password with
.BR \-\-password ,
.BR CTRIPLE_PASSWORD ,
or at the prompt (only offered on a terminal).
.TP
.BI "no access to " /dev/bus/usb/...
.B manager
cannot write the unit's USB node: run it with sudo, or install the package's
udev rule, which gives the node to the user at the seat.
.TP
.B gs_usb has not taken it
The unit became the adapter but no interfaces appeared: the udev rule is not
installed, or another file of the same name in
.I /etc/udev/rules.d
masks it.
.TP
.B pyserial is missing
Install
.BR python3\-serial .
.TP
.IB port ": could not open port"
Another program (the Device Manager, a terminal, a script) has the serial
port open.
.TP
.B bringing an interface up needs root
Run
.BR up " or " down
with sudo.
.TP
.BI "no bit rate for " interface
.B up
found no bit rate on the command line or in the configuration file.
.TP
.BI "ip link set " "...: error"
.BR ip (8)
refused; its own message follows. "Invalid argument" with a bit rate the
unit's controller cannot make, or with a data rate and
.B fd
not offered by this kernel.
.SH EXAMPLES
What is plugged in:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ ctriple
4f002b: the Device Manager's port /dev/ttyACM0, firmware 2.0.10; starts as the Manager port
.RE
.fi
.PP
The adapter, every power-up, all three buses at 500 kbit/s:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ ctriple socketcan \-\-persist
$ sudo ctriple up \-\-bitrate 500000
$ candump ct4f002b\-can1
.RE
.fi
.PP
CAN2 as CAN FD at 500 kbit/s and 2 Mbit/s, CAN3 only listening at 250 kbit/s:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sudo ctriple up \-\-bus 2 \-\-bitrate 500000 \-\-dbitrate 2000000
$ sudo ctriple up \-\-bus 3 \-\-bitrate 250000 \-\-listen\-only
.RE
.fi
.PP
The same with
.BR ip (8),
and an exact sample point:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sudo ip link set ct4f002b\-can2 down
$ sudo ip link set ct4f002b\-can2 type can bitrate 500000 sample\-point 0.8 \e
      dbitrate 2000000 dsample\-point 0.75 fd on
$ sudo ip link set ct4f002b\-can2 up
.RE
.fi
.PP
A test with nothing wired:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sudo ctriple up \-\-bitrate 500000 \-\-loopback
$ candump ct4f002b\-can1 & cansend ct4f002b\-can1 123#DEADBEEF
.RE
.fi
.PP
Two units, one of them chosen:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ ctriple socketcan \-\-unit 4f002b
.RE
.fi
.PP
Back to the Device Manager, and stay there:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ ctriple manager \-\-persist
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cantriple\-socketcan (7),
.BR ip\-link (8),
.BR candump (1),
.BR cansend (1),
.BR cangen (1)
